      "writtenOn": "2025-03-06T14:51:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The process of setting `filter_level` to zero begins when the current frame’s `filter_level[0]` and `filter_level[1]` are selected as zero by the filter search. Any frame referring to this frame can also have its `filter_level` set to zero, subject to filter thresholds, minimum filter_level of reference frames and pyramid level.\n\nThis logic applies only to frames with `pyramid_level` \u003e 1 (i.e., internal ARF frames) and when the minimum filter level among reference frames is zero. For a given frame, `filter_level` selected is closer to 0 (from search_filter_level()) indicating that the frame is closer to the original reference with less blocking artifacts. The logic is developed based on the fact that if the deblocking filter is skipped for such a frame, coding impact may not be significant.\n\nAs you correctly pointed out, this may create a chain reaction, but the frames coded in future may get a chance to recover, as the filter level search of the current frame happens around the original `filter_level` of the previous frame. The filter level is only set to zero if all four components `(lf-\u003efilter_level[0], lf-\u003efilter_level[1], lf-\u003efilter_level_u, and lf-\u003efilter_level_v)` fall below the threshold.",
